From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: VectraOS toolchain <toolchain@vectraos.invalid>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 06:14:54 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] [VectraOS] RISC-V: swifttailcc and swiftasync (stage 3)

The RFC's stage 3 (discourse 91560), which upstream hasn't started:
clang accepts swiftasynccall on riscv (not the E ABIs), so swiftc lowers
async functions as swifttailcc with musttail. The backend passes
swiftasync in x22 (s6), as AArch64 does; a swifttailcc function preserves
none of x20-x22 (CSR_*_SwiftTail), so a tail call can pass new ones.

Guaranteed tail calls: a swifttailcc function pops its incoming stack
arguments (16-byte aligned) when it returns, and a caller's ADJCALLSTACKUP
takes the pop back. A tail call between swifttailcc functions may pass any
stack arguments: they are stored FPDiff (caller's incoming bytes minus the
callee's) from the caller's incoming area, as fixed objects, after the loads
of incoming arguments they overlap (addTokenForArgument, after AArch64's);
the tail call is PseudoTAIL_SA or PseudoTAILIndirect_SA, whose operand the
epilogue applies to the stack pointer once the frame is gone, before making
it a plain PseudoTAIL*, so nothing later sees the new pseudos. Below the
incoming stack pointer the fixed objects reserve the space at the top of the
frame; the split SP adjustment, shrink-wrapping, save/restore libcalls and
push/pop are off for swifttailcc. Incoming stack arguments are mutable there.
Not supported: indirect, sret or byval arguments in a musttail call (fatal,
as before), and a moving tail call with Zicfilp or KCFI.
